<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Latin League</h1><p class="st-meta">650 BCE – 338 BCE (approx.) · capital Aricia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Alliance of the Latin towns around Rome, bound by shared cults and rights, that fought and then partnered Rome until Rome dissolved it in 338 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> peoples of the plain south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> shared a language, the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jupiter-latiaris">Jupiter Latiaris</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cavo">Alban Mount</a> and other common sanctuaries from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. By the 6th century BCE they met in council at the grove of Ferentina and dedicated a federal shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diana-mythology">Diana</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aricia">Aricia</a>, whose founding a Latin dictator, Egerius Baebius of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tusculum">Tusculum</a>, is said to have led.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The League was a loose association of towns such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tusculum">Tusculum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aricia">Aricia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanuvium">Lanuvium</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibur">Tibur</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/praeneste">Praeneste</a>, sometimes led by an elected dictator. Members shared rights of trade and intermarriage with each other. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latins">Latins</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-regillus">Lake Regillus</a> around 496 BCE, the treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spurius-cassius-vecellinus">Spurius Cassius</a> in 493 BCE made Rome and the League equal partners in defence, and they jointly founded colonies against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aequi">Aequi</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/volsci">Volsci</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> came to dominate the alliance, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> revolted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-war">Latin War</a> of 340–338 BCE. Rome won, dissolved the League in 338 BCE and made separate treaties with each town. The shared 'Latin rights' survived as a legal status Rome later granted to colonies and provinces.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/latin-league.jpg" alt="Latin League" width="320" height="264"><figcaption>Image: Cassius Ahenobarbus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ligue-latine-carte.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 BCE – 338 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aricia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Latin Confederation, Foedus Latinum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-494&amp;focus=latin-league" aria-label="Latin League on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4989 691 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Latin League lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5309 947L5308 947M5309 947L5304 942M5309 947L5309 945M5309 947L5314 944M5309 947L5311 940"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5308 947h0M5304 942h0M5309 945h0M5314 944h0M5311 940h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5309 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5309 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Aricia. <a href="/atlas?year=-494&amp;focus=latin-league">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
